Exactly. Naming something doesn’t make it real. And even when something is real, the name doesn’t guarantee we’ve understood it correctly.

Karma is a perfect example.

People often talk about it as though the universe maintains a moral accounting system: do good, receive good; do evil, receive punishment.

But reality doesn’t reliably operate that way. Good people suffer. Terrible people prosper. Some actions have consequences decades later. Others never produce any recognizable justice.

And yet there are real mechanisms hiding underneath what people call karma.

* Reciprocity: How you treat people can influence how they treat you.
* Reputation: Past actions can change your future opportunities.
* Feedback loops: Behaviors can create conditions that reinforce those same behaviors.
* Selection effects: We notice satisfying examples of justice while overlooking cases where nothing happens.
* Chance: Sometimes outcomes have little to do with anyone’s virtue or wrongdoing.

These mechanisms can generate karma-like patterns without requiring a supernatural system of cosmic justice.

And I think your observation reaches much further.

Naming versus knowing

There’s a sequence worth distinguishing:

\text{Phenomenon}\rightarrow\text{Observation}\rightarrow\text{Concept}\rightarrow\text{Name}

But humans frequently reason backward:

\text{Name}\rightarrow\text{Assumed concept}\rightarrow\text{Assumed reality}

We invent a word, begin treating it as an explanation, and eventually forget that the explanation still needs to be tested.

Think about words like luck, destiny, intelligence, ego, justice, or even nature.

They’re useful compression tools. But each can conceal multiple different mechanisms, some verified, some hypothetical, and some contradictory.

The invariant

A label compresses a model of reality. It does not certify the model’s truth.

And the reverse matters just as much:

Something doesn’t have to be named, recognized, or believed in to be real.

Gravity operated before anyone formulated a theory of it. Previously unknown biological processes affected living organisms long before scientists identified them.

Likewise, a named concept could refer to something real while its popular explanation remains wrong.

For Pantheon, I’d call the corresponding discipline semantic de-reification: temporarily stripping away a concept’s familiar name and asking what observable mechanisms, if any, remain.

And there’s one more layer I think you’d appreciate:

Even a false belief can produce real consequences.

Someone who believes in karma might behave more generously. Someone else might use that belief to blame people for suffering they didn’t deserve.

The supernatural claim could remain unverified while the psychological and social effects of believing it are measurable.

So there are really three different questions:

Is the thing real? Is our explanation correct? And what does believing it cause us to do?

Three different truths—or possible errors—hiding under one little word.

And that circles back to your observation about roads: sometimes what we call the cause is only the name we’ve given to a pattern generated by something much deeper.